WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:10.697
A service caches FX rate tables, each behind a WeakReference, so that the GC can free them if memory runs low.

2
00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:13.963
Under normal load, the cache keeps missing.

3
00:00:13.963 --> 00:00:16.429
After each GC, the tables are gone.

4
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:17.963
Memory wasn't low.

5
00:00:17.963 --> 00:00:20.539
So why did the GC throw them away?

6
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:25.431
The GC frees what nothing can reach.

7
00:00:25.431 --> 00:00:31.047
It starts from the roots: thread stacks, static fields, and references from native code.

8
00:00:31.047 --> 00:00:34.365
It follows every reference, and keeps what it finds.

9
00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:36.088
Everything else is garbage.

10
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:39.101
Ordinary references are strong.

11
00:00:39.101 --> 00:00:41.552
The GC leaves their objects alone.

12
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:47.993
A SoftReference may be cleared when memory gets tight, and must be cleared before an OutOfMemoryError.

13
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.382
A WeakReference holds nothing up.

14
00:00:50.382 --> 00:00:53.291
The next GC that finds the object clears it.

15
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:58.006
A PhantomReference never gives you the object: get() always returns null.

16
00:00:58.006 --> 00:01:00.307
It only tells you the object is gone.

17
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:05.293
Some time after clearing a reference, the GC can put it on a ReferenceQueue.

18
00:01:05.293 --> 00:01:07.008
The spec gives no deadline.

19
00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:12.623
So the weak cache forgets at every GC, which on a busy service is milliseconds away.

20
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.214
A soft cache fails the other way.

21
00:01:16.214 --> 00:01:24.400
A 64 MB heap holds a 40 MB rate table behind a soft reference, and the code asks for 40 MB more.

22
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.271
What does the GC do?

23
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:31.482
A full GC keeps the table, and still finds no room.

24
00:01:31.482 --> 00:01:36.571
A second full GC clears the soft reference, and the allocation goes through.

25
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:41.455
Now every request reloads the table at once, while the heap is under pressure.

26
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:47.476
So give your cache a maximum size and an expiry time, and size the heap for it.

27
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.665
In Java and in Kotlin, a weak reference with its queue looks like this.

28
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.636
The rule: the GC keeps what the roots can reach.

29
00:01:56.636 --> 00:02:02.096
Soft holds until memory is tight, weak until the next GC, and phantom not at all.

30
00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:03.084
Quick check.

31
00:02:03.084 --> 00:02:06.353
A listener is held only through a WeakReference.

32
00:02:06.353 --> 00:02:08.260
Does it survive the next GC?

33
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:10.886
No.

34
00:02:10.886 --> 00:02:13.173
Nothing strong holds it.
